1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,439
Can you show me Docker in CI?

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,106
In a team, nobody builds production images on a laptop.

3
00:00:12,106 --> 00:00:16,512
A CI pipeline does it on every change, always the same way:

4
00:00:16,512 --> 00:00:20,767
test the code, build the image, smoke-test the container,

5
00:00:20,767 --> 00:00:27,487
and only then push it to a registry with an immutable tag (the version or the Git commit).

6
00:00:27,487 --> 00:00:30,249
If any step fails, nothing is pushed.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,959
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

8
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:38,796
Read the pipeline first:

9
00:00:38,796 --> 00:00:41,762
it is four commands and a loop.

10
00:00:41,762 --> 00:00:44,154
Run it for version 1.0.0:

11
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:47,107
Run it for version 1.0.0:

12
00:00:47,107 --> 00:00:50,285
The registry now has the version:

13
00:00:50,833 --> 00:00:53,448
The registry now has the version:

14
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:56,685
Let me try something.

15
00:00:56,685 --> 00:01:01,565
A teammate changes the greeting and pushes without running the tests:

16
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:04,300
The pipeline stopped at step 1:

17
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:11,541
the RUN node --test step of the test stage failed, so docker build failed and set -e ended the script.

18
00:01:11,541 --> 00:01:12,889
Nothing was pushed:

19
00:01:14,633 --> 00:01:22,361
Either the change is wrong (revert it), or the contract really changes (update the test in the same commit).

20
00:01:22,361 --> 00:01:24,508
Here the change was a mistake:

21
00:01:26,267 --> 00:01:26,980
Got it.

22
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:29,731
What do I need to remember?

23
00:01:30,267 --> 00:01:31,649
A Docker pipeline:

24
00:01:31,649 --> 00:01:37,026
test → build → smoke test → push, and a failing step stops everything.

25
00:01:37,567 --> 00:01:43,951
A multi-stage Dockerfile gives the tests their own stage; the shipped image contains no tests.

26
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:52,750
Tag with immutable versions (version, commit); deploy by tag or, even safer, by digest.

27
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:58,804
I keep hearing about build and push with GitHub Actions.

28
00:01:58,804 --> 00:02:00,265
What is it, really?

29
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:04,916
How the pipeline of lesson 135 looks in a real CI system:

30
00:02:04,916 --> 00:02:10,694
a GitHub Actions workflow that tests and builds the image on every pull request,

31
00:02:10,694 --> 00:02:17,988
and pushes it to the GitHub Container Registry (ghcr.io) on every push to main and every version tag.

32
00:02:17,988 --> 00:02:25,210
The workflow logs in with the job's own short-lived GITHUB_TOKEN, so no password is stored anywhere.

33
00:02:25,210 --> 00:02:33,660
This lesson keeps the GitHub Actions details to what Docker needs; the workflow syntax itself belongs to a CI course.

34
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:39,359
First, a fresh practice folder for this lesson.

35
00:02:39,900 --> 00:02:46,660
The workflow's actions, each pinned to a full commit SHA (a tag like v7 could be moved to different code):

